  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfter a beautiful day today, widespread chances for rain\nincrease once again for tomorrow as our current high begins to\nshift to the east and a low pressure system approaches from the\nwest. By tomorrow afternoon, HiRes guidance indicates two\ninitial areas of showers spreading into eastern New York: one to\nthe northwest of Albany ahead of a surface wave, and another to\nthe southwest associated with the incoming system's warm front.\nOnce the warm sector begins to enter our CWA, increasing warm\nair and moisture advection will help to fill in the gap between\nthese two areas of showers, leading to more widespread coverage\nby tomorrow evening. Rain showers will then persist through\ntomorrow night into at least Monday morning, but potentially as\nlate as Monday afternoon, as the parent low and associated\nshortwave aloft swing by to our south. The period of greatest\nrainfall will occur tomorrow night into early Monday morning.\n\nGenerally, most of the rainfall rates contained within the\nshowers tomorrow through Monday morning will be light. In fact, the\nlatest HREF only shows isolated to highly scattered areas of \n10-20% probabilities for at least 0.25\" of rain per hour. That\nsaid, there will be decent forcing for ascent as a result of\nthe causing system in addition to high precipitable water\ncontent (PWATs ~1.6\" to 2\" which are 2-2.5 STDEVs above normal).\nTherefore, embedded moderate to locally heavy downpours cannot\nbe ruled out. And as we have been largely dry so far this month,\nthe 0.25\" to local 1\" rainfall amounts that are anticipated \nshould be beneficial and not cause any widespread flooding \nissues. This will be especially true if the system takes as\nsoutherly a track and remains as progressive as the current\nguidance would suggest. However, should the system slow and/or\nshift a bit farther north, such that the heaviest rain sets up\nover areas south of Albany, there could be an isolated instance\nof flash flooding. This could also lead to locally higher\nrainfall amounts, particularly in the Catskills, of up to 2\".",
